// Arm SVE target layouts.
//
// Layout ids are target-owned mapping results, not `#micro.layout` enumerants.
// Machine facts are read only through machine queries, so nothing here names a
// concrete executor. Both domains stay finite: VW enumerates exactly the three
// widths the 512-bit profile advertises, so the solver's search is bounded and
// a dtype the profile does not model has no answer instead of a guessed one.

// Plain row-major: the logical index is the physical index. It deliberately
// takes no size parameter -- a parameter no constraint reads would enlarge the
// solve without adding legality.
layout sve.row_major() {
  require rank == 2;
  implements row_major;
  map (m, n) -> (m, n);
}

// Inner-dimension blocking whose VW follows the machine's vector width for the
// operand's *own* element type: f32 selects 16, bf16 32, i8 64, and f64 has no
// lane fact, so the solve errors rather than picking a width. VW describes
// physical blocking; it is not an assertion that every logical tile dimension
// equals a lane count. Small and remainder tiles can pad under this static map,
// and no executable masking behavior is claimed.
layout sve.blocked_2d(int VW) {
  param VW in {16, 32, 64};
  require rank == 2;
  require VW == machine.compute("vector_engine").lanes(element_type);
  implements blocked;
  map (m, n) -> (m, floordiv(n, VW), mod(n, VW));
}
